  <page_number>59</page_number>
  <running_head>GRAMM. GRÆCA.</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1" para="true">ἀγυλος abscessus inter nares &amp; majorem</l>
    <l n="2">angulum oculorum.</l>
    <l n="3" para="true">αὐλὸς vestibulum, vel janua vestibuli.</l>
    <l n="4" para="true">ἀσφαλτος bitumen.</l>
    <l n="5" para="true">ἀμαθος (quod &amp; ἀμαθος) arena.</l>
    <l n="6" para="true">ἄμμος sabulum: quod ἐξ ἄμμος.</l>
    <l n="7" para="true">ἀμπελος vitis. sic compos.</l>
    <l n="8" para="true">ἀμπελάμπελος, vel ἀμπελίαμπελος, (per syncopen poet.) vitis agrestis, labrusca.</l>
    <l n="9" para="true">ἀμφοδος bivium, circuitus.</l>
    <l n="10" para="true">ἀνοδος ascensus: vel reditus, iteratio.</l>
    <l n="11" para="true">ἀπλατος, sive ἀπλατὸς, callis, semita trita.</l>
    <l n="12" para="true">εἰσοδος introitus.</l>
    <l n="13" para="true">εἰρεος remigatio.</l>
    <l n="14" para="true">ἐρημος solitudo.</l>
    <l n="15" para="true">ἐπάνοδος reditus.</l>
    <l n="16" para="true">ἡπειρος, &amp; Doricé ἡπειρος, continens.</l>
    <l n="17" para="true">ἵππος equitatus, vel equa: pro equo vero</l>
    <l n="18">est masculinum.</l>
    <l n="19" para="true">ὄαφος uxor: pro confabulatione masculi-</l>
    <l n="20">num est.</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Greek words are transcribed as best legible from a soft, faded scan; accents and breathings are approximate. Several Greek lines are uncertain, notably the first word of each entry.</notes>
